!! Invoking the Bundle Creator Tool from CMake

As part of the CMake build system support, a custom function `poco_osp_bundle`
is provided, which takes care of invoking the Bundle Creator tool appropriately.
The function is defined in `PocoOspBundle.cmake`, which can be found in the
top-level `cmake` directory. An example `CMakeLists.txt` for building a bundle
(from the ServiceListener sample) is shown below:

    set(TARGET "com.appinf.osp.samples.servicelistener")
    set(BUNDLE_SPEC "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/ServiceListener.bndlspec")

    # Sources
    file(GLOB TARGET_SRCS "src/*.cpp")

    # Dynamic Library
    poco_osp_bundle_module(${TARGET} ${TARGET_SRCS})

    target_link_libraries(${TARGET} PRIVATE Poco::OSP Poco::Zip Poco::Util Poco::XML Poco::JSON Poco::Foundation)

    # Build Bundle
    poco_osp_bundle(${TARGET}-bundle
        DEPENDS ${TARGET}
        SPEC ${BUNDLE_SPEC}
        CODE $<TARGET_FILE:${TARGET}>
        DESTINATION ${POCO_OSP_BUNDLE_DIR}
        INSTALL_COMPONENT Samples
    )
----

The function `poco_osp_bundle_module`, also defined in `PocoOspBundle.cmake`, adds
the library of the bundle as a <[MODULE]> library with the file name that OSP loads:
the target name without a prefix, with the suffix of Poco::SharedLibrary::suffix()
(<[.dylib]> on macOS, preceded by <[d]> in a Debug build). The function does not add
the <[d]>: the build of poco-osp sets <[CMAKE_DEBUG_POSTFIX]> to <[d]>, and a project
that builds Debug bundles sets it too.

With CMake, it's recommended to specify the binaries that should go into the bundle
on the command-line and not in the bundle specification file's `<code>` element,
as shown in the above example. The <[CODE]> argument of `poco_osp_bundle` is passed
to the Bundle Creator tool with the <[--code]> option, and the <[SYMLINKS]> option
passes <[--symlinks]>. The function runs the tool in the directory of the bundle
specification file, unless another directory is given with <[WORKING_DIRECTORY]>,
and makes the bundle depend on the files in the <[bundle]> directory of that
directory. With <[INSTALL_COMPONENT]>, the bundle file is installed into
<[POCO_INSTALL_BUNDLEDIR]> (<[bundles]> if it is not set) with that install component.
<[POCO_OSP_BUNDLE_DIR]> is the directory into which the build of poco-osp writes
the bundles, <[${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/${POCO_INSTALL_BUNDLEDIR}]> unless it is set.

In the build of poco-osp, `cmake/PocoOSPComponents.cmake` includes `PocoOspBundle.cmake`.
A project that uses an installed poco-osp gets `poco_osp_bundle`, `poco_osp_bundle_module`
and `poco_remote_gen` (see below) with

    find_package(Poco REQUIRED COMPONENTS OSPBundleCreator)
----

which also provides the Bundle Creator tool as the imported target
<[Poco::OSP::BundleCreator]>.


!! Generating Sources with RemoteGenNG from CMake

The function `poco_remote_gen`, defined in `PocoOspRemoteGen.cmake` in the top-level
`cmake` directory, runs RemoteGenNG, the code generator of the POCO RemotingNG library,
at configure time, so that the generated sources can be added to a target:

    poco_remote_gen(<configuration> GEN_DIR_NAME <directory> [DEFINES <property>...])
----

The caller sets <[POCO_REMOTEGEN_NG]> to the RemoteGenNG command; the configure stops
if it is empty. A name without a directory is looked up in <[PATH]>, and a relative
directory is relative to <[CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR]>, as is a relative path of the
configuration file (<[<configuration>]>). RemoteGenNG gets the option <[compiler]> with
the value <[msvc]>, <[gcc]> or <[clang]> from <[CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID]>; for another
compiler, the configure stops. It also gets the properties <[DEFINES]>
(<[<name>=<value>]>, or <[<name>]> for an empty value), <[CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR]>,
<[POCO_OSP_BASE]> if it is defined, and <[RemoteGen.output.timestamps=false]>, which keeps
the content of an unchanged output the same, except for <[<Activator>Impl.cpp]> (see
below). <[POCO_OSP_BASE]> defaults to the root of the poco-osp source tree when the
module is the one in that tree; a configuration names
the OSP headers with it, and its own headers with <[CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR]>, for example
<[${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/include/Service.h]>.

RemoteGenNG runs in the directory <[remotegen-<id>]> of <[CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR]>,
which is emptied before it generates, so the output directories of the configuration
are relative to it. When a generation completes, the files that the previous generation
wrote and this one does not are removed, and each file written is copied to the same
relative path under <[CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR]> if its content differs; a file with the
same content keeps its time and is not compiled again. A file that cannot be removed or
copied stops the configure, and the next configure generates again. With
<[RemoteGen.output.osp.bundleActivator]>, every generation writes
<[<Activator>Impl.cpp]> anew, with the time of the generation (to the second) in its
header, so the file is normally copied, and compiled again if it is a source of a
target. The edited implementation is kept in the source tree.

<[GEN_DIR_NAME]>, relative to <[CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR]>, is the directory that the
configuration writes to. Generated files are replaced and removed in it, so the
configure stops if it is or contains the source or binary directory (top-level or
current), or the directory of the configuration file or of the file it resolves to,
each as named and with symbolic links resolved.

A RemoteGenNG whose help names the option <[list-dependencies]> lists at every configure
the headers that it reads and the directories that decide them. It generates if the
output directory does not exist, if the build directory has no completed generation of
the call, if a listed header does not exist, if the command or the list differs from
the one of the last completed generation, or if the configuration, a listed header or
the RemoteGenNG executable (if <[POCO_REMOTEGEN_NG]> names it with a directory) is newer
than the start of that generation. The configuration and the listed paths are
dependencies of the build, so a change of them configures again. Without the option,
RemoteGenNG generates at every configure, the configuration is the only dependency,
and a warning says so.

The configuration file or a listed path that contains a "[", "]", ";", double quote, "$",
"|", control character or (not on Windows) backslash is not a dependency of the build, nor
is a listed directory that is or is in the build directory or the binary directory of the
call, as named or with symbolic links resolved; a warning names them. A change of them
runs RemoteGenNG only at the next configure.

Example, for a configuration <[RemoteGen.xml]> whose output directories are
<[generated/include]> and <[generated/src]>:

    poco_remote_gen(RemoteGen.xml GEN_DIR_NAME generated)
    file(GLOB GENERATED_SRCS "${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/generated/src/*.cpp")
    target_sources(${TARGET} PRIVATE ${GENERATED_SRCS})
    target_include_directories(${TARGET} PRIVATE ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/generated/include)
----


!! Bundles and Cross-Compiling for Embedded Linux

When building bundles for a target system with a different processor architecture than the host system
(cross-compiling), a few things must be taken care of. First, inside a bundle, executable code (shared
libraries) are stored in a directory with the path <[bin/<osname>/<osarch>]>, where <[<osname>]>
specified the operating sytem name (in case of an embedded Linux system, this is <[Linux]>), and
<[<osarch>]> specifies the processor architecture. The processor architecture must match the
output of the command <[uname -m]> on the target system.

This can be accomplished in two ways. The first is to use a bundle specification file where,
at the <[code]> element, you explicitely specify the target operating system name and architecture
using the <[platform]> attribute. Example:

      <code platform="Linux/armv5tejl">
          bin/Linux/armv5tejl/*.so
      </code>
----

Alternatively, you can omit the <[platform]> attribute, and instead specify the target operating
system name and architecture as command line arguments to the Bundle Creator tool. This is done
using the <[--osname]> (or <[-n]>), and <[--osarch]> (or <[-a]>) command line arguments.

Note that when cross-compiling an OSP application, you must build the POCO and OSP libraries and
executables twice -- once for the host system, and once for the target system. A CMake build for
the target system (<[CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING]>) creates the bundles with the <*bundle*> program found
in <[PATH]>, which must be the Bundle Creator tool of the host build. The `poco_osp_bundle` function
passes the CMake variables <[OSP_OSNAME]> and <[OSP_OSARCH]> to the tool as <[--osname]> and
<[--osarch]>. Unless they are set, <[OSP_OSNAME]> is <[Windows_NT]> for Windows and
<[CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME]> for other systems, and <[OSP_OSARCH]> is <[CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR]>.
Set <[OSP_OSARCH]> where <[CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR]> differs from the output of <[uname -m]>
on the target system.

!! Stripping Shared Libraries and Executables

Some build systems, e.g. Yocto, build all shared libraries and executables with debug
symbols and only remove (strip) the debug symbols in a later install or packaging step.
This is too late for use with the Bundle Creator tool, which means that bundles
would contain shared libraries and executables with debug information. This is 
undesirable, especially for use on embedded systems with limited resources.

To solve this issue, the Bundle Creator tool can optionally strip debug symbols
from all shared libraries and executables incorporated into a bundle (via the
<[code]> element in the bundle specification file, or the respective command-line option). 
This happens after the files have been copied into the temporary directory from which they 
are copied into the bundle. Therefore the original files are not modified. A symbolic link that
is copied with <[--symlinks]> is not stripped, since its target can be outside the bundle.

To enable this feature, the <[--strip]> (or <[/strip]> on Windows) command-line option
can be used. The name of the strip tool (usually <[strip]>, with the usual prefix for cross-compiling)
must be specified. Alternatively, the environment variable <[BUNDLE_STRIP]> can be set
to the name of the tool.
